#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221114 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d2b186-64d2-5ad8-b168-6c7bea413df3" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using Euler's Method for Second-Order ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "81a8347f-7852-5413-8d96-946ec658929e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21664" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y'' + 4xy' - 3y = 2x, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad y'(0) = -2$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z = y'$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nUse the information above to approximate the value of $y(1)$." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(a) = c, \\\\ z^{′} = f(x, y, z)\\end{vmatrix}z(a) = d\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″} + 4xy^{′} - 3y &= 2x \\\\\ny^{″} &= -4xy^{′} + 3y + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable\n\n$$\nz = y', z' = y''\n$$\n\nin the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = -4xz + 3y + 2x\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n\n$$\nz(0) = y'(0) = -2\n$$\n\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(0) = 1, \\\\ z^{′} =-4xz + 3y + 2x,\\end{vmatrix}z(0) =-2\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=-2$, we place $y=1$ and $z=-2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= z =-2 \\\\\nz^{′} &= -4xz + 3y + 2x \\\\\n&=-4 \\cdot 0 \\cdot (-2) + 3 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0 + 3 + 0 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ |  | $-2$ | $3$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (-2) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-2 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$, $\\Delta y$ to $y$, and $\\Delta z$ to $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + (-2) =-1 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz =-2 + 3 = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx -1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221117 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd3b7856-4ac5-5473-a585-949dd06100e8" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/id #uuid "62004d73-b9ca-5133-b016-e2694a55211c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21668" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$x^2y'' + 2y' = 4x^2, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\qquad y'(1) = -1$\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)$ and $y'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(a) = c, \\\\ z^{′} = f(x, y, z)\\end{vmatrix}z(a) = d\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}y^{″} + 2y^{′} &= 4x^{2} \\\\\nx^{2}y^{″} &= 4x^{2} - 2y^{′} \\\\\ny^{″} &= \\frac{4x^{2} - 2y^{′}}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable\n\n$$\nz = y', z' = y''\n$$\n\nin the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{4x^2 - 2z}{x^2}\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n\n$$\nz(1) = y'(1) = -1\n$$\n\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(1) =-1, \\\\ z^{′} = \\frac{4x^{2} - 2z}{x^{2}},\\end{vmatrix}z(1) =-1\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and $y'=z$ at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nwe will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac32 - 1 = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= z =-1 \\\\\nz^{′} &= \\frac{4x^{2} - 2z}{x^{2}} \\\\\n&= (4 \\cdot 1^{2} - 2 \\cdot (-1))/(1^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 + 2}{1} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$, $\\Delta y$ to $y$, and $\\Delta z$ to $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy =-1 + (-\\frac{1}{2}) =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz =-1 + 3 = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac32$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx -\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221118 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785f39df-2c61-53b8-9293-093915b24356" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/id #uuid "25d6bf3b-282e-5c8b-b665-923dd602849d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21669" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$2x y'' - (y')^2 + 4y = 0, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\quad y'(1) = 2$\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(3)$ and $y'(3)$." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(a) = c, \\\\ z^{′} = f(x, y, z)\\end{vmatrix}z(a) = d\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2xy^{″} - (y^{′})^{2} + 4y &= 0 \\\\\n2xy^{″} &= (y^{′})^{2} - 4y \\\\\ny^{″} &= ((y^{′})^{2} - 4y)/(2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y'$, $z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{z^2 - 4y}{2x}\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n\n$$\nz(1) = y'(1) = 2\n$$\n\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(1) =-1, \\\\ z^{′} = \\frac{z^{2} - 4y}{2x},\\end{vmatrix}z(1) = 2\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z=y'$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3$. The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2$, and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3 - 1}2 = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=2$.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y$, $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new}$, $y_\\text{new}$, and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= z = 2 \\\\\nz^{′} &= \\frac{z^{2} - 4y}{2x} = (2^{2} - 4(-1))/(2 \\cdot 1) = 4 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx = 4 \\cdot 1 = 4 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + 1 = 2 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy =-1 + 2 = 1 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz = 2 + 4 = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y$, $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the second row), and the values of $x_\\text{new}$, $y_\\text{new}$, and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= z = 6 \\\\\nz^{′} &= \\frac{z^{2} - 4y}{2x} = \\frac{6^{2} - 4 \\cdot 1}{2 \\cdot 2} = 8 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 6 \\cdot 1 = 6 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx = 8 \\cdot 1 = 8 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 2 + 1 = 3 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + 6 = 7 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz = 6 + 8 = 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ | $6$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |  | $14$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(3) \\approx 7\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny'(3) = z(3) \\approx 14\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd3b7856-4ac5-5473-a585-949dd06100e8" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "09a8cd04-2f26-5e5d-a576-d02938b0d056" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220310} {:answer-field/id #uuid "3a58a557-6e3d-5674-ac1a-19bb8ed43152" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220311}] :question/id #uuid "585a6b31-349e-5195-b6c5-30ce9e2bf96b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332989" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$xy^{″} + 3yy^{′} = 2x, y(1) = 0, y^{′}(1) =-2$\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{5}{4})$ and $y^{′}(\\frac{5}{4})$.$y(\\frac{5}{4}) \\approx$\n{{field-1}},\n$y^{′}(\\frac{5}{4}) \\approx$\n{{field-2}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"approximating-the-solution-to-a-second-order-ode-using-eulers-method-with-two-steps\"></a>"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785f39df-2c61-53b8-9293-093915b24356" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8618804a-4b7c-5789-b925-56ea9f7e57a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220329} {:answer/id #uuid "d97e13c4-07dd-5049-aebb-63791f485843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(11)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220330} {:answer/id #uuid "3971dca9-8c74-5120-a2a9-36a4fa0b8043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220331}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f464a18e-9605-5490-9e7d-f8558d0ac485" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb3c8b5f-6f96-5815-95b9-f941928bbdd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (1)/(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220333} {:answer/id #uuid "271b9473-2e1e-5926-9899-aeb75a741a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220334} {:answer/id #uuid "42479b77-6dfe-5f3b-ad4c-a613c849ab28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220335}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cde5ad59-ed92-59e2-b6b7-ad6b896c6dfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220332}] :question/id #uuid "f387b360-2a40-587c-949a-4f9384d87f91" :question/math-academy-id "q-333374" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the following initial value problem:\n$x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} + y = 0, y(1) =-2, y^{′}(1) = 1$\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and $y^{′}(2)$.$-1$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd3b7856-4ac5-5473-a585-949dd06100e8" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "b9572349-9e73-5f53-87a2-625332ffbd62" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220542} {:answer-field/id #uuid "f019e598-fb2a-53d7-b68e-cfae193834c4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220543}] :question/id #uuid "c5d59cc7-44b7-5133-bc4e-ec03309ee9ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-333724" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$xy^{″} + 2yy^{′} = 3x, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) =-1$\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and $y^{′}(2)$.$y(2) \\approx$\n{{field-1}},\n$y^{′}(2) \\approx$\n{{field-2}}"}]}
